\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One irreducible module on opposite circle modes}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a circle of period #{N\ge3} take a field as in
\ref{fdip-016E}. Let the group of order two act by reversal,
and choose a residue #{v} with #{2v\ne0}. Its orbit consists
of #{v,-v} by \ref{fdip-0165}, and its stabilizer in the
reversal group is trivial. The two scalar fields #{\alpha_v}
and #{\alpha_{-v}} span the module of \ref{fdip-016F}
induced from the one-dimensional space at #{v}. A unit shift
multiplies them by #{\zeta^v} and #{\zeta^{-v}}, respectively,
where #{\zeta} is the chosen primitive #{N}th root. Reversal
exchanges them. A nonzero invariant subspace contains at least
one of these lines by the translation projections, hence both
by reversal. Their span is irreducible even though the two shift
multipliers differ.}
